        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A wide crack is not a narrow crack that needs more filler. Past half an inch it needs a floor — closed-cell backer rod pressed in so the filler sits a quarter to half an inch deep on top of it. That single step is the difference between a repair that holds for years and one that slumps, sinks and splits by spring.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Past an inch, change products entirely. Pourable rubberized filler cannot behave properly in a wide opening; cold patch compacted in shallow layers can. And if what you are looking at is an interconnected web rather than discrete lines, stop — that is base failure, and no filler addresses it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then give it time. Clear and dry the crack properly, fill slightly proud, strike level, and let the filler reach full cure before any sealer goes over the top. Nearly every crack repair that fails inside a season failed at the preparation stage, not at the product stage.
          </p>
